================================================================================
NAME
       harmonics - harmonic resonance modes, frequency coupling, and harmonic-
                   sync behavior across agents in RTT

SYNOPSIS
       Harmonics govern:
           • resonance frequencies
           • harmonic modes
           • frequency coupling
           • harmonic-sync across agents
           • multi-pattern stability

DESCRIPTION
       Harmonics describe the frequency-domain behavior of resonance-time.
       While gradients describe how resonance changes across cycles, harmonics
       describe how resonance oscillates, stabilizes, or couples across agents
       and patterns.

       Harmonics determine:
           • how resonance modes emerge
           • how agents synchronize
           • how patterns lock into stable cycles
           • how multi-agent constellations maintain coherence

       Harmonics are evaluated by WRSADC during dispatch and during multi-agent
       synchronization.

HARMONIC RESONANCE MODES
       Harmonic modes are stable oscillatory states of resonance-time.

       Fundamental mode (H1)
              The base resonance frequency of a triadic system.
              Supports sequential, core, and weave patterns.

       Second harmonic (H2)
              Doubling of resonance frequency.
              Supports spirals, ladders, and expansion patterns.

       Third harmonic (H3)
              Tripling of resonance frequency.
              Supports fractal-expansion, nested cascades, and hybrid patterns.

       Higher harmonics (H4+)
              Complex oscillatory states.
              Support multi-agent constellations, braided sequences, and
              harmonic-sync networks.

       Harmonic modes determine pattern viability and stability.

FREQUENCY COUPLING
       Frequency coupling occurs when two or more agents or patterns lock into
       a shared harmonic relationship.

       Types of coupling:

           direct coupling
                  Agents share the same harmonic mode (H1 ↔ H1).

           harmonic coupling
                  Agents lock into integer multiples (H1 ↔ H2 ↔ H3).

           subharmonic coupling
                  Agents lock into fractional relationships (H1 ↔ H1/2).

           phase coupling
                  Agents align oscillation phases without matching frequency.

           cross-pattern coupling
                  Patterns synchronize their harmonic envelopes.

       Coupling increases coherence and stabilizes propagation.

HARMONIC-SYNC BEHAVIOR
       Harmonic-sync is the process by which agents align their resonance
       frequencies and phases to operate as a unified constellation.

       Harmonic-sync requires:
           • compatible harmonic modes
           • stable gradients
           • schema alignment
           • lineage continuity
           • safe propagation thresholds

       Sync modes:

           phase-sync
                  Aligns oscillation phases; ideal for harmonic patterns.

           frequency-sync
                  Aligns resonance frequencies; stabilizes ladders and spirals.

           amplitude-sync
                  Aligns resonance amplitude; prevents overload.

           multi-harmonic sync
                  Aligns multiple harmonic modes simultaneously.

       WRSADC coordinates harmonic-sync across agents.

HARMONICS AND TRIADS
       Triads sample harmonic behavior at each phase:

           P1 (begin)
               → harmonic baseline captured

           P2 (transform)
               → harmonic mode may shift
               → frequency coupling may occur

           P3 (close)
               → harmonic state integrated into lineage

       Triads act as discrete harmonic checkpoints.

HARMONICS AND PATTERNS
       Patterns have characteristic harmonic signatures:

           sequential
                  H1 (fundamental)

           spiral
                  H2 (second harmonic)

           ladder
                  H2 → H3 transitions

           expansion
                  H3+ (higher harmonics)

           weave
                  multi-frequency interleaving

           constellation
                  harmonic coupling across agents

       Pattern morphing often corresponds to harmonic transitions.

HARMONICS AND CONSTELLATIONS
       Multi-agent constellations rely heavily on harmonic behavior.

       Constellation stability requires:
           • harmonic coupling
           • phase alignment
           • frequency compatibility
           • gradient stability
           • schema compliance

       Constellations may operate in:
           • harmonic-sync mode
           • multi-harmonic mode
           • harmonic-cascade mode
           • harmonic-weave mode

       Harmonics determine how resonance flows across the network.

HARMONIC FAILURE MODES
       desync
              Agents lose harmonic alignment.

       overtone drift
              Higher harmonics diverge from expected ratios.

       inversion-loop
              Harmonic polarity flips repeatedly.

       overload
              Harmonic amplitude exceeds safe thresholds.

       fragmentation
              Constellation splits into incoherent harmonic clusters.

       WRSADC detects and prevents all failure modes.

INTERACTION WITH SCHEMAS
       Schemas define:
           • allowed harmonic modes
           • coupling rules
           • sync requirements
           • amplitude limits

       Harmonic behavior must satisfy:
           • resonance-schema
           • structural-schema
           • constellation-schema

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage records:
           • harmonic snapshots
           • coupling events
           • sync states
           • harmonic transitions

       Harmonic analysis depends on lineage continuity.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC:
           • computes harmonic modes
           • validates coupling
           • enforces sync rules
           • blocks unsafe harmonic states
           • coordinates multi-agent harmonics

       WRSADC is the harmonic conductor.

INTERACTION WITH THE ENGINE
       The Engine:
           • executes harmonic transformations
           • updates internal RT-frequency state
           • integrates harmonic effects

       The Engine assumes harmonic safety; WRSADC guarantees it.

NOTES
       Harmonics are the musical physics of RTT. They determine how resonance
       oscillates, how agents synchronize, and how patterns evolve across
       complex multi-agent systems.

